      "category": "system",
      "gap": "No information about which communication channel or tool the childcare provider uses to send notices (e.g., a dedicated app, email, paper notes, SMS group).",
      "why_it_matters": "The channel determines whether automation is technically feasible and what integration points exist."
    },
    {
      "category": "decision",
      "gap": "No criteria specified for deciding when a message requires an active response versus when it can simply be read and acknowledged.",
      "why_it_matters": "Without a decision rule, an automated system cannot distinguish messages that need action from those that do not."
    },
    {
      "category": "output",
      "gap": "It is unclear where or how special events and items-to-bring are recorded so the household can act on them (e.g., a shared calendar, a to-do list, a reminder app).",
      "why_it_matters": "The storage and notification mechanism for reminders is essential to assess whether this step can be automated end-to-end."
    },
    {
      "category": "actor",
      "gap": "It is not stated which household member is responsible for reading messages, responding, and setting reminders, or whether this responsibility is shared.",
      "why_it_matters": "Knowing who owns each step clarifies handoff points and whether automation would serve one person or need to notify multiple people."
    },
    {
      "category": "frequency",
      "gap": "The volume and regularity of childcare messages is unknown (e.g., daily digests, ad-hoc notices, weekly newsletters).",
      "why_it_matters": "Frequency affects the priority and return-on-investment of automating the reading and triage steps."
    },
    {
      "category": "exception",
      "gap": "No information is provided about what happens when a response deadline is missed or when a required item cannot be provided.",
      "why_it_matters": "Exception handling logic is needed to design a robust automated workflow that escalates or flags failures appropriately."
    }
  ],
  "clarification_questions": [
    {
      "id": "Q1",
      "question": "What channel or tool does the childcare provider use to send notices and messages to your household — for example, a dedicated family communication app, email, SMS, or paper notes sent home with the child?",
      "targets": ["system"]
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "question": "How do you currently decide whether a message needs an active reply versus just needs to be read — is there a specific type of content, a keyword, or a request format that signals a response is required?",
      "targets": ["decision"]
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "question": "Once you identify a special event or an item to bring, where do you record it so the household remembers — for example, a shared calendar, a reminder app, a whiteboard, or a messaging thread?",
      "targets": ["output"]
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "Who in the household is responsible for checking childcare messages and acting on them — is it one designated person, or do multiple caregivers share that responsibility?",
      "targets": ["actor"]
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5",
      "question": "Roughly how many childcare messages or notices does your household receive per week, and do they arrive on a predictable schedule or at unpredictable times?",
      "targets": ["frequency"]
    },
    {
      "id": "Q6",
      "question": "What happens if a response deadline is missed or your household realises too late that a required item needs to be brought — is there a fallback step, and who handles it?",
      "targets": ["exception"]
    }
  ]
}
